---
title: Proposition 22
---
order: 22
---
short_label: III.22
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: The sum of the opposite angles of quadrilaterals in circles equals two right angles.
----
proof:

Let {ABCD} be a circle, and let {ABCD|ABCDq} be a quadrilateral in it.

I say that the sum of the opposite angles equals two right angles.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="260" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.22 diagram" src="propIII22.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce names the circle and the quadrilateral both ABCD, and the
    // element name belongs to the circle (his figure). The quadrilateral is
    // ABCDq, a transparent polygon, and the prose reaches it through the
    // display override {ABCD|ABCDq}. The same collision recurs one level
    // down: "the triangle ABC" and "the angle ABC" — the triangle owns the
    // name (transparent polygon ABC), the marker is angABC, and every caption
    // and prose mention of the angle is {ABC|angABC}, as in II.13.
    //
    // His figure draws the six lines only. Everything added is transparent
    // (;0;0;0;0 polygons and arcs) or an angle marker, hidden in the
    // resting figure (showAngles defaults false), so the static figure is
    // unchanged. Segments are carried by their arcs: BADC is the major arc
    // from B round through A and D to C, ADCB the major arc from A through D
    // and C to B; both endpoint orders were chosen by measuring which arc the
    // bundle draws (an arc element draws the minor or the major arc
    // according to its endpoint order — the sign #172 is about).
    //
    // Colour: equal angles share a colour — orange CAB = BDC, teal BCA =
    // ADB; the opposite pair the proof adds, ABC and ADC, are magenta; the
    // pair the "similarly" covers, BAD and DCB, green. Named colours give an
    // opaque face, so at A, C and D the containing wedge is declared before
    // the wedges inside it and the radii are pinned (22 inside, 34 outside).
    var CIRC = ["ABCD","A","B","C","D"];
    var QUAD = CIRC.concat(["AB","BC","CD","AD","ABCDq"]);
    var OPP  = QUAD.concat(["angABC","angADC"]);
    var DIAG = OPP.concat(["AC","BD"]);
    var TRI  = DIAG.concat(["ABC","angCAB","angBCA"]);
    var SEG1 = TRI.concat(["angBDC","segBADC"]);
    var SEG2 = SEG1.concat(["angADB","segADCB"]);
    // The "similarly" beat: the triangle's markers and the segments come off,
    // the other opposite pair comes on.
    var SIM  = DIAG.concat(["angBAD","angDCB"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.22",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;50,50",
            "B;point;free;200,40",
            "C;point;free;200,180",
            "ABCD;circle;circumcircle;A,B,C;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "D;point;circleSlider;ABCD,120,200",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            // The quadrilateral and the triangle the proof names; the lines
            // above already draw every side. Explicit ;0 faces.
            "ABCDq;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;0;0",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0;0",
            // The two segments, as their (major) arcs.
            "segBADC;sector;arc;B,A,C;0;0;0;0",
            "segADCB;sector;arc;A,D,B;0;0;0;0",
            // Angle markers, containing wedge first at each vertex.
            "angBAD;sector;angleMarker;A,B,D,34;0;0;green;green",
            "angCAB;sector;angleMarker;A,C,B,22;0;0;orange;orange",
            "angABC;sector;angleMarker;B,A,C;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angDCB;sector;angleMarker;C,D,B,34;0;0;green;green",
            "angBCA;sector;angleMarker;C,B,A,22;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angADC;sector;angleMarker;D,A,C,34;0;0;magenta;magenta",
            "angADB;sector;angleMarker;D,A,B,22;0;0;teal;teal",
            "angBDC;sector;angleMarker;D,B,C,22;0;0;orange;orange"
        ],
        // Draggables (checked against the bundle: A, B, C, D) are the four
        // letters of the given circle and quadrilateral, all introduced on
        // slide 1 — nothing to defer, and no derived points to list.
        aliases: { "BA": "AB", "CA": "AC", "DA": "AD", "CB": "BC", "DB": "BD", "DC": "CD",
                   "CAB": "angCAB", "BAC": "angCAB",
                   "BCA": "angBCA", "ACB": "angBCA",
                   "BDC": "angBDC", "CDB": "angBDC",
                   "ADB": "angADB", "BDA": "angADB",
                   "ADC": "angADC", "CDA": "angADC",
                   "BAD": "angBAD", "DAB": "angBAD",
                   "DCB": "angDCB", "BCD": "angDCB",
                   "BADC": "segBADC", "ADCB": "segADCB" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”).
            { text: "The sum of the opposite angles of quadrilaterals in circles equals two right angles.",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.22" }] },
            { text: "Let {ABCD} be a circle,",
              visible: CIRC,
              highlighted: ["ABCD"] },
            { text: "and let {ABCD|ABCDq} be a quadrilateral in it.",
              visible: QUAD,
              highlighted: ["ABCDq"] },
            { text: "I say that the sum of the opposite angles equals two right angles.",
              visible: OPP,
              highlighted: ["angABC", "angADC"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angADC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Join {AC} and {BD}.",
              visible: DIAG,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "BD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            { text: "Then, since in any triangle the sum of the three angles equals two right angles, the sum of the three angles {CAB}, {ABC|angABC}, and {BCA} of the triangle {ABC} equals two right angles.",
              visible: TRI,
              // The triangle and its two new markers are the animation
              // targets; angABC, already on since slide 3, is not, and keeps
              // the gold as the third angle of the sum.
              highlighted: ["angABC"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "ABC",    name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "angCAB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBCA", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the three angles of a triangle", ref: "I.32" } ] },
            { text: "But the angle {CAB} equals the angle {BDC}, for they are in the same segment {BADC},",
              visible: SEG1,
              highlighted: ["angCAB","segBADC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBDC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "angles in the same segment", ref: "III.21" } ] },
            { text: "and the angle {ACB} equals the angle {ADB}, for they are in the same segment {ADCB},",
              visible: SEG2,
              highlighted: ["angBCA","segADCB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angADB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "angles in the same segment", ref: "III.21" } ] },
            { text: "therefore the whole angle {ADC} equals the sum of the angles {BAC} and {ACB}.",
              visible: SEG2,
              highlighted: ["angADC","angCAB","angBCA"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "ADC is the sum of BDC and ADB", ref: "C.N.2" } ] },
            { text: "Add the angle {ABC|angABC} to each.",
              visible: SEG2,
              highlighted: ["angABC"] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC}, {BAC}, and {ACB} equals the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ADC}.",
              visible: SEG2,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angCAB","angBCA","angADC"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the angle ABC added to each", ref: "C.N.2" } ] },
            { text: "But the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC}, {BAC}, and {ACB} equals two right angles, therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ADC} also equal two right angles.",
              visible: SEG2,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angADC"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "the three angles of the triangle ABC", ref: "I.32" },
                  { claim: "equal to the same two right angles",    ref: "C.N.1" } ] },
            { text: "Similarly we can prove that the sum of the angles {BAD} and {DCB} also equals two right angles.",
              visible: SIM,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angBAD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angDCB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the opposite angles of quadrilaterals in circles equals two right angles.",
              visible: SIM,
              highlighted: ["angABC","angADC","angBAD","angDCB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.22") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.32":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI32/",
        "III.21":   "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII21/",
        "C.N.1":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Join {AC} and {BD}.

Then, since in any triangle the sum of the three angles equals two right angles, the sum of the three angles {CAB}, {ABC|angABC}, and {BCA} of the triangle {ABC} equals two right angles. [!just I.32]

But the angle {CAB} equals the angle {BDC}, for they are in the same segment {BADC}, and the angle {ACB} equals the angle {ADB}, for they are in the same segment {ADCB}, therefore the whole angle {ADC} equals the sum of the angles {BAC} and {ACB}. [!just III.21]

Add the angle {ABC|angABC} to each. Therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC}, {BAC}, and {ACB} equals the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ADC}.  But the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC}, {BAC}, and {ACB} equals two right angles, therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC} and {ADC} also equal two right angles.

Similarly we can prove that the sum of the angles {BAD} and {DCB} also equals two right angles.

Therefore *the sum of the opposite angles of quadrilaterals in circles equals two right angles.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
